ile scenarios, including on AWS EBS.  RocksDB's file deletions on ext4, combined with `fstrim`, immediately return storage to the host, unlike Btrfs' CoW behavior. While lacking a \"device add\" method like Btrfs, ext4 supports online block-level expansion using a specific tool, provided the backing disk or image is resized.\n\n\nExtending ext4 on AWS EBS involves resizing the volume, then informing the kernel of the new size, updating the partition table, and finally, expanding the filesystem online using a specific tool, all without downtime.  Sparse image files on the host are easily enlarged with a dedicated command, consuming no additional space until data is written. LVM on ext4 offers a mechanism to effectively add storage like btrfs' \"device add\" functionality, utilizing volume groups, logical volumes, and filesystem resizing. Employing sparse over-allocation is the recommended cloud strategy; create large virtual extents initially, only paying for actual data written, minimizing the need for frequent resizing.\n\n\n"}
